<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Bakr ibn Umar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1087">1087</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Bakr ibn Umar was an Almoravid leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamtuna">Lamtuna</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> in the eleventh century. After leaving northern operations to his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a>, he returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and campaigned in the south. He is associated with Almoravid pressure on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a> and with spreading the movement's influence in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ibn-umar.jpg" alt="Abu Bakr ibn Umar" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: alif · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abu_Bakr_ibn_Omar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Bakr belonged to the Banu Turgut, a clan of the Lamtuna Berbers. His uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ibrahim">Yahya ibn Ibrahim</a>, chieftain of the Lamtuna, had launched the Almoravid movement in the early 1040s with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-ibn-yasin">Abdallah ibn Yasin</a>. When Yahya ibn Umar was killed at the Battle of Tabfarilla in the spring of 1056, Abdallah ibn Yasin appointed Abu Bakr military commander and amir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In late 1056 Abu Bakr recaptured Sijilmassa from the Maghrawa, then secured the routes of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, taking the Draa valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Sous</a>, with its capital Taroudannt, in 1057. An alliance with the Masmuda Berbers let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">High Atlas</a> and take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghmat">Aghmat</a> in 1058. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-ibn-yasin">Abdallah ibn Yasin</a> was killed among the Berghwata of western Morocco, Abu Bakr conquered them in a brutal campaign of revenge. Now sole leader, he claimed no religious authority and took the modest title amir al-Muslimin, Prince of the Muslims.</p><p>He married Zaynab an-Nafzawiyyah, the wealthiest woman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghmat">Aghmat</a>, but found life in the town stifling. Around 1060 he and his lieutenants camped on the pastures by the Tensift River, and the camp grew into the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, whose founding is credited to him. While his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> reduced the Zenata strongholds of the north, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> in 1070, discontent spread among the desert clans. Abu Bakr went south to quell it, divorcing Zaynab, who then married Yusuf. When he returned in 1072, Yusuf persuaded him with gifts of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> to leave the north to him, though he kept Abu Bakr's name on the coinage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mauritanian oral tradition holds that he was killed by a poisoned arrow shot by an old, blind Gangara chieftain in the pass of Khma, on the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, and places his grave at Maksam in the Tagant region; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> tradition names a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> bowman, Amar Godomat, near Lake Rkiz. The date of 1087 follows the chronicler Ibn Abi Zar and is supported by coins minted at Sijilmassa in his name until then. Oral tradition also makes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndiadiane-ndiaye">Ndiadiane Ndiaye</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof Empire</a>, his son, though the dates make a direct link impossible.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Bakr ibn Umar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In West Africa, the southern branch under <b>Abu Bakr ibn Umar</b> helped spread Maliki Islam through the Sahel, contributing to the Islamisation of the western Sudan and shaping the religious character of the later Mali and Songhai empires.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of the Almoravid leader <b>Abu Bakr ibn Umar</b> in 1087 the Soninke recovered their independence, but trade routes were already shifting eastward towards the Niger bend, and Ghana no longer held the position that had made it supreme.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Tagant, in what is now <a href="/countries/mauritania">Mauritania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.56,-11.43&amp;year=1077" aria-label="Tagant on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4240 1476 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Abu Bakr ibn Umar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4690 1746h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4690 1746h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=18.56,-11.43&amp;year=1077">What was here in 1077? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
